-- SZA (Survive Zombie Arena) module for Serenity. ROUGH DRAFT / PROTOTYPE, built from an
-- actual client-side dump of the place file (Potassium/Dex export: place 98927955463992
-- Arena.rbxlx), not guesswork.
--
-- Confirmed from the dump: the real Buy Health button lives at
-- PlayerGui.MainGui.ControlPanel2.HealthUpgrade (a TextButton -- cost "25", heals to "100"
-- per its own child labels), and there's a matching RemoteEvent at ReplicatedStorage.
-- Remotes.UpgradeRemotes.PurchaseHealthUpgrade, but no decompiled client script anywhere in
-- the dump actually calls that remote (with whatever arguments it expects), so its call
-- signature isn't guessed at all here -- this clicks the real button instead and lets the
-- game's own already-working handler take it from there, same approach used everywhere else
-- in this hub whenever a remote's arguments aren't confirmed. The "press C to buy" keybind
-- mentioned wasn't found in any decompiled script either (no literal KeyCode.C reference
-- turned up anywhere in the dump), so it's not simulated -- clicking the real button works
-- regardless of whatever key the game itself has bound to it.
--
-- Ability Spam is the opposite situation: no dedicated ability-cast GUI button turned up
-- anywhere under MainGui (its top level is all shop/currency UI -- Minerals, gear upgrades,
-- Buy Health, robux offers), and no literal "KeyCode.Q/E/R" text turned up in any decompiled
-- script either -- plausible if the real binding builds Enum.KeyCode[key] from a config
-- table rather than writing the enum out literally, which wouldn't show up as that exact
-- text. Real ability plumbing IS confirmed to exist (ReplicatedStorage.Remotes has a
-- RemoteEvent literally named "Ability", plus per-class ability ModuleScripts like
-- WorldEnderAbilityClient/StarShooterAbilityClient, and Souls has its own SoulsChanged/
-- GetSouls remotes matching what you described), just not its exact call signature or
-- keybind wiring. Given that and no clickable button to fall back to, this simulates the
-- actual Q/E/R keypresses instead -- same keypress/keyrelease -> VirtualInputManager
-- fallback chain already used successfully in UE and Animal_Hospital -- so it works
-- regardless of the underlying binding mechanism, same reasoning as clicking a real button.

